{"chunk_id":"d68437","wall_time_seconds":0.000007875,"exit_code":0,"original_token_count":2862,"output":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 2862)\nTotal output lines: 45\n\n---\nname: cameragr-no-self-retouch\ndescription: \"Koco visuals come from GPT, not from me — every new screen is a skeuomorphic GPT-drawn object (my concept), never code-drawn or web/menu-like; no retouching of art, no code-drawn parts\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n  originSessionId: a7b155bd-1e03-4921-b49c-c714f27f4dc7\n  modified: 2026-11-20T03:19:42.457Z\n---\n\nOn 2026-11-19 I removed a small white ▶ mark from the door plate with cv2 inpainting; the user objected: \"你不要去这样改啊 然后你让 GPT 去生图啊\". Any change to the content of an art asset — even erasing a tiny label or seam — is to be done by GPT: I prepare the input image and a prompt, the user runs it and sends the result back.\n\nLater the same day I drew the shutter's iris blades in SwiftUI; the user said: \"你也别自己拿程序生…你去研究逻辑，然后让 GPT 去生素材，这样好看一些\". So visible parts that move are also GPT art: I work out the mechanism and geometry, give GPT a silhouette template with known pivot/centre, and animate the sprite it returns.\n\nOn 2026-11-21, after I built the fold-open film library in plain SwiftUI cards and buttons, the user set a standing rule: \"所有新的界面不要自己用程序画 你来出创意 gpt 作图 要求拟物化 尽量不要像网页或者菜单 这条记住\". Every new screen (the film library, the waist-level finder, any future page) starts as my creative concept of a real physical object — a film drawer, a light box with a contact sheet, a camera hood — drawn by GPT through /codex-image; code only places live content (photos, text, viewfinder) into the measured rects of that art and handles touch. Flat card lists, pill buttons and menu rows are what to avoid.\n\n**Why:** my retouches
